# A chart, drawn by a React component and baked to frames.
#
# `component:` is a part that is not drawn by this program at all. The file
# beside this one is plain JavaScript; cuttr renders it in the WebKit that is
# already on the machine, one frame at a time, into
# `.cuttr/components/walks/` — and from there it is a folder of PNGs like any
# other, composited by the same code that composites a logo.
#
#   cuttr-render examples/components/chart.cuttrproj
#
# The first render bakes and takes a few seconds longer; every render after it
# reuses the frames. Nothing is installed, nothing is fetched, and there is no
# `node_modules`: React is in the app bundle, at 143 kB.
#
# ## Why this earns the machinery
#
# Count what is on the frame: twelve bars, twelve values, twelve month names,
# five gridlines with a number on each, a total counting up, and a sentence
# naming the best month. Every position is arithmetic on the data — the bar
# width falls out of how many months there are, the gridlines out of the
# ceiling, each bar's height out of its own value against that ceiling.
#
# As a `scenes:` block that is about forty parts and a hundred and twenty keys,
# and it is a file holding *the result of a layout* rather than the layout —
# which is the thing `examples/scenes/typing.cuttrproj` warns about at nine
# parts. Change one month's number and every bar after it has to be re-typed by
# hand. Here, one number in an array changes and the chart re-lays itself.
#
# That is the whole test for whether a component is the right answer: **is the
# position of the thing a fact about the data?** For a title it is not — a title
# goes where somebody decided it looks right, and `scenes:` says that in text
# that diffs. For a chart it is, and no amount of keyframes will make it so.
#
# ## What this costs, said plainly
#
# The frames are the artefact and they are not small — 200 PNGs, a few megabytes.
# They live beside the project so that they travel with it, because the picture
# depends on the WebKit that drew it and a machine with a different macOS would
# otherwise render a different chart. `--rebake` is how you ask for this
# machine's opinion instead.
#
# The scene editor cannot edit it. It is one rectangle of pixels with nothing
# inside it, so it can be moved, sized and faded and nothing else — which is
# fine for something that is the whole frame, and is why the keys below say
# `width: 1, height: 1` and then leave it alone.
#
# And a scene with a sequence in it is painted *into* the picture rather than
# laid over it, which means it sits under every caption and every other scene
# however this list is arranged. That is deliberate — `docs/remotion.md` gives
# the measurement — and for a chart it is what anybody would have wanted.
cuttr-project: 1

output:
  size: 1920x1080
  fps:  25
  file: chart.mov

timeline:
  # Eight seconds, which is the component's `duration:` — and they have to
  # agree. A part on for longer than its bake holds the last frame, the way a
  # keyframe holds its last value; a part on for less is cut off where it is.
  - card:  00:08.000
    as:    chart
    fill:  ["#141b2b", "#070a12"]

overlays:
  - scene: year
    from:  "@chart"
    in:    {fade: true, over: 0.4}
    out:   {fade: true, over: 0.5}

scenes:
  # One part, the whole frame. The ground is on the card rather than in here for
  # the reason `examples/effects/celebrate.cuttrproj` gives at length: a scene is
  # drawn over the picture, so a full-frame background in it would hide the
  # picture. The component's own frames are transparent everywhere it did not
  # draw, which is what lets the card's gradient show through.
  year:
    parts:
      - component: walks.js
        duration:  8
        props:     {accent: "#7fd4ff", unit: km}
        keys:
          - {t: 0, x: 0.5, y: 0.5, width: 1, height: 1, opacity: 1}
